{ println } :: import("std/fmt");
// `Array(T, N).fill` is `fill : (fn(comptime(val) : T) -> comptime(Self))` — it
// builds the whole array at COMPILE time, so its argument must be compile-time
// known. `i32.default()` is an ordinary run-time call
// (`impl(i32, Default(default : (fn() -> Self)(i32(0))))` declares a plain
// `fn`), so passing it is a genuine user error.
//
// Before the fix that error was never reported: a run-time value arrives as
// `.Some(UnknownVal)` rather than `.None`, sailing past the "compile-time
// known" guard into the arm that manufactures an `UnknownVal` for the whole
// array. The definition's evaluation was then swallowed and the program
// aborted at RUN time out of a binary that compiled rc=0.
//
// Generics are NOT involved — that was the original mis-diagnosis. The
// variable is literal-versus-call.
// WORKS: a compile-time known fill value.
ok :: (fn() -> Array(i32, usize(3)))(Array(i32, usize(3)).fill(i32(0)));
// WORKS: a generic fill with a comptime value — the def-time trial sees an
// unbound `T` here, so the rejection must stay SOFT inside a trial or this
// stops specializing.
ok_generic :: (fn(generic(T : Type), x : T, where(T <: Comptime)) -> Array(T, usize(3)))(
  Array(T, usize(3)).fill(T(0))
);
// REJECTED (was: abort stub at run time). Uncomment to see the diagnostic.
//   broken :: (fn() -> Array(i32, usize(3)))(Array(i32, usize(3)).fill(i32.default()));
//
// The same shape through a plain user function fails identically, which is
// what shows the trait is irrelevant:
//   z :: (fn() -> i32)(i32(0));
//   broken2 :: (fn() -> Array(i32, usize(3)))(Array(i32, usize(3)).fill(z()));
main :: (fn() -> unit)({
  println(`${ok().len()}`);
  println(`${ok_generic(i32(0)).len()}`);
});
export(main);
